Local modifications:
  - dkms.conf: adds PATCH[0..2]/PATCH_MATCH[0..2] to apply the local hardening
    patches during the DKMS build (see patches/). DKMS 3.2.2 resolves the
    PATCH[] value below its patches/ directory, so the value is the bare file
    name (e.g. 0001-t2smc-range-checks.patch).  Patch numbers 0002 and 0004 are
    intentionally absent (dropped, upstream fixed them; see the disposition table).
    The C source stays pristine so upstream re-syncs remain byte-diffable.
  - patches/0001-t2smc-range-checks.patch: local hardening (fan min/target range
    checks against the factory F%dMn/F%dMx envelope captured at probe, RTC offset
    range check, modulo-and-sign-extend fold).  Adds t2smc_range.h at the DKMS
    module root at build time only (56-t2-dkms.sh flattens src/ into
    /usr/src/t2smc-0.2/, so the patch is written with -p1 paths at the module
    root).  The fan validator captures the factory envelope so the min cannot
    ratchet upward, validates the 64-bit hwmon long before narrowing it to
    unsigned int, and does not read F%dTg for a target write.  The envelope
    capture is non-fatal: if the SMC cannot report the limits the write path
    falls back to the live F%dMn/F%dMx.  A fan target of 0 is NOT range-checked:
    it skips the range check and takes upstream's own hand-back path
    (clear fan_manual, F%dMd = 0), because upstream now defines target 0 as
    "return the fan to SMC control" (513a82d); no speed is written for it.
    The charge-limit and temperature-allocation validators that earlier versions
    of this patch carried were removed with the dropped patches 0004/0002
    (and from tests/test_t2smc_range.c).
  - patches/0003-t2smc-cache-init.patch: initialise the cache-fill return
    value in t2smc_get_entry_by_index() so the locked goto-out does not
    read an uninitialised int when a second caller observes the cache
    already populated.
  - patches/0005-t2smc-telemetry-robust.patch: saturate the float-telemetry
    shift before it exceeds the 64-bit range (t2smc_read_scaled), and stop reading from an
    uninitialised value16/signed16/val in t2smc_power_show() (every read-failure
    path now returns before the value is used; upstream's trailing common
    `if (ret)` check stays for the cases that do not touch val first).
  - tests/test_t2smc_range.c: userspace tests for t2smc_range.h (see
    build/test-t2smc-range.sh).
  - PROVENANCE: packaging metadata, not an upstream file.

Per-patch disposition against 8c7f725 (2026-11-14 refresh):
  0001-t2smc-range-checks         KEPT, REBASED.  6 of 11 hunks did not apply (struct
                                  t2smc_device, init_keycache, hwmon_write, devm cleanup, ...);
                                  merged by hand onto the new structure.  Upstream still has
                                  no fan range validation (hwmon_write only rejects val < 0).
                                  Single behaviour adaptation: target 0 skips the range check
                                  (see above), since the old patch's "reject below factory min"
                                  would have blocked upstream's new hand-back-to-SMC feature.
                                  Dead helpers t2smc_temp_alloc_count and t2smc_charge_limit_valid
                                  removed from t2smc_range.h with their patches.
  0002-t2smc-temp-discovery-idx   DROPPED, upstream contains the fix.  Defect was: pass one
                                  sized temp_keys by its success count while pass two could
                                  index past it.  Upstream rewrote discovery as
                                  t2smc_discover_sensors(): ONE pass, kcalloc(end - begin)
                                  (the whole candidate range, what the patch added), and
                                  set->count = n written after the loop (the patch's
                                  temp_count = idx).  The two-pass code the patch edited no
                                  longer exists (`grep -n "temp_count"` finds nothing).
  0003-t2smc-cache-init           KEPT, applies unchanged.  Upstream
                                  t2smc_get_entry_by_index() still declares `int ret;` and
                                  reaches `goto out` -> `if (ret)` with it unset when the
                                  entry became valid while waiting for the mutex.
  0004-t2smc-charge-limit-atomic  DROPPED, upstream contains the fix by a rewrite (3deea3b +
                                  497badc).  Patch goals and where upstream meets each:
                                  (a) validate before the first write: t2smc_set_charge_limit()
                                  returns -EINVAL for val < 1 || val > 100 before any SMC
                                  access; (b) reject values CHWA-only hardware cannot
                                  represent: the CHWA/BCLM write path is gone (the key CHWA does
                                  not occur in the source); the only write is CHLS;
                                  (c) serialise the BCLM+CHLS/CHWA transaction under a
                                  dedicated charge_lock: the transaction no longer exists
                                  (single CHLS write) and t2smc_set_charge_limit(),
                                  t2smc_write_chls(), t2smc_read_charge_limit() and the
                                  suspended-store path all run under charge_lock
                                  (lockdep_assert_held in the helpers); (d) the getter takes
                                  the same lock: charge_limit_show() does.  Behavioural
                                  difference vs the old driver (upstream's choice): limit 0 is
                                  now rejected, 1..100 accepted.
  0005-t2smc-telemetry-robust     KEPT, hunk 1 (read_scaled float shift) applies unchanged
                                  (code identical upstream); hunk 2 (power_show) REBASED by hand:
                                  upstream added cases, so every original case plus the
                                  new BATTERY_POWER negation (`val = -val`) and the
                                  TIME_TO_EMPTY/FULL conversion now return on error before
                                  touching val/value16.  Residual note: the saturation path
                                  returns the most negative value for an out-of-range negative
                                  exponent and upstream's BATTERY_POWER then negates it (the
                                  kernel build wraps this; only out-of-range input reaches it).
